    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco.jpg" alt="Guglielmo Embriaco" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco">Guglielmo Embriaco</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genova-Palazzo_San_Giorgio-DSCF7708.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Lodovico Pogliaghi, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> was an old Ligurian port, squeezed between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apennines">Apennines</a> and the sea, which had suffered Muslim raids in the tenth century and by the eleventh was raiding back, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> in attacks on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdia">Mahdia</a> in 1087. Around 1099 its leading citizens bound themselves by oath into a sworn association, the <em>compagna communis</em>, led by elected consuls; this is conventionally taken as the birth of the commune. The timing was not accidental. Genoese fleets took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-crusade">First Crusade</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-embriaco">Guglielmo Embriaco</a> built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siege-engine">siege engines</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in 1099 and helped take Caesarea in 1101, bringing back booty that included the green glass bowl revered as the <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacro-catino">Sacro Catino</a></em>. In return the crusader princes granted Genoa quarters, tax exemptions and a share of revenues in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antioch">Antioch</a> and other ports. The young commune also fought for the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, joining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-vii-of-leon-and-castile">Alfonso VII of Castile</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV of Barcelona</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tartus">Tortosa</a> in 1146–1148. These campaigns left Genoa heavily in debt, an early sign of a lasting pattern: public enterprise financed by private creditors. The city’s official chronicle, begun by the consul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caffaro-di-rustico-da-caschifellone">Caffaro di Rustico da Caschifellone</a>, recorded these early decades from the commune’s own point of view.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/genoa"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/genoa.jpg" alt="Genoa" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Piazza_de_Ferrari,_Genoa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Julian Lupyan, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> had little farmland, and its wealth came from the sea. Its merchants carried grain, salt, alum, wine, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a> and slaves between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, Provence, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>. The register of the notary <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-scriba">Giovanni Scriba</a>, covering 1154–1164, is the oldest surviving notarial cartulary in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and shows the <em>commenda</em> contracts by which investors entrusted goods and capital to travelling partners. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-nymphaeum-1261">Treaty of Nymphaeum</a> of 1261 with the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-viii-palaiologos">Michael VIII Palaiologos</a>, Genoa gained privileged access to the Black Sea, its colony at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Pera</a> faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a> became a hub for grain, fish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> and slaves from the steppe. Alum from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phocaea">Phocaea</a> and mastic from Chios brought large profits. Society was dominated by great clans, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/doria-family">Doria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spinola-family">Spinola</a>, Fieschi and Grimaldi among them, organised from the fourteenth century in <em>alberghi</em>, associations of related families sharing a name and a neighbourhood. Genoese finance was precocious: public debt was divided into transferable shares, marine insurance contracts appear in the fourteenth century, and in the sixteenth century the city’s bankers became the principal lenders of the Spanish crown. The city’s population probably reached 60,000 or more around 1300 before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a>, which by most accounts first reached Europe on ships from the Black Sea in 1347.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-meloria-1284"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-meloria-1284.jpg" alt="Battle of Meloria (1284)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-meloria-1284">Battle of Meloria (1284)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:How_the_Genoese_discomfited_the_Pisans_at_Meloria.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>’s power rested on its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> and on the readiness of its citizens and great families to arm ships. Its long rivalry with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> and trade ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-meloria-1284">Battle of Meloria</a> in 1284, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-doria">Oberto Doria</a> destroyed the Pisan fleet and took thousands of prisoners, crippling Pisa as a naval power. The contest with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> was longer. Genoa backed the restoration of Byzantine rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> in 1261, and in the war that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lamba-doria">Lamba Doria</a> defeated a Venetian fleet at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-curzola">Battle of Curzola</a> in 1298; according to tradition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> was among the captives and dictated his travels in a Genoese prison. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a> (1378–1381) a Genoese fleet under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pietro-doria">Pietro Doria</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chioggia">Chioggia</a> at the edge of the Venetian lagoon in 1379, but was itself blockaded and forced to surrender in 1380, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-turin-1381">Peace of Turin</a> of 1381 ended Genoese hopes of supremacy. Genoese crossbowmen served as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, notably for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-crecy">Crécy</a> in 1346, and Genoese admirals commanded the fleets of foreign kings. In 1453 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-giustiniani-longo">Giovanni Giustiniani Longo</a> led the defence of the land walls of Constantinople against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a>. From 1528 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a>’s galleys served <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> corsairs, but in later centuries Genoa’s small navy could not protect it from the French bombardment of 1684.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/oberto-spinola"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/oberto-spinola.jpg" alt="Oberto Spinola" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/oberto-spinola">Oberto Spinola</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coa_fam_ITA_spinola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Massimop, CC BY-SA 3.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The commune was governed at first by consuls chosen from the leading families. Their feuds led to the appointment from 1191 of a <em>podestà</em>, an outside magistrate serving a fixed term, and later to captains of the people such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guglielmo-boccanegra">Guglielmo Boccanegra</a> (1257–1262) and the joint rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-doria">Oberto Doria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oberto-spinola">Oberto Spinola</a> from 1270. In 1339 a popular revolt made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simone-boccanegra">Simone Boccanegra</a> the first doge, elected for life and supposed to come from the non-noble families. Factional struggle between Guelph and Ghibelline clans and between nobles and <em>popolari</em> remained endemic, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> repeatedly submitted to foreign lords: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-of-france">Charles VI of France</a> in 1396–1409, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-visconti">Visconti of Milan</a> in 1421–1435 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-sforza">Sforza</a> after 1464. A distinctive institution was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-saint-george">Bank of Saint George</a>, founded in 1407 to consolidate the public debt, which collected state revenues and even governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> on behalf of the republic; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> thought it a state within the state. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrea-doria">Andrea Doria</a>’s reform of 1528 created an aristocratic republic: the nobility was grouped into 28 <em>alberghi</em>, doges served two-year terms, and power lay with the councils and collegi of the nobility. Disputes between old and new nobles led to brief civil war and to the new laws of 1576, which governed the republic until its end.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/strada-nuova"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strada-nuova.jpg" alt="Strada Nuova" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/strada-nuova">Strada Nuova</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Genova-DSCF7468.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Twice25 &amp; Rinina25, CC BY 2.5</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> was one of the creators of the commercial economy of medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its notarial records, partnership contracts, insurance and public debt shares make it a key case for historians of business and finance, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bank-of-saint-george">Bank of Saint George</a> is often cited among the first public banks. Its colonies, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Pera</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> to Chios and Famagusta, linked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> with the steppe and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and Genoese sailors pushed into the Atlantic: the Vivaldi brothers set out to sail around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> in 1291 and were never seen again, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lanzarotto-malocello">Lanzarotto Malocello</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canary-islands">Canary Islands</a> in the early fourteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>, born in Genoa around 1451, came out of this world of Genoese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic trade</a>, and Genoese merchants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> financed early Spanish voyages and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> economy of the Atlantic islands. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernand-braudel">Fernand Braudel</a> spoke of an age of the Genoese from 1557 to 1627, when Genoese bankers handled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a> and the exchange fairs at Piacenza became the clearing house of European finance. The city’s legacy is visible in the palaces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strada-nuova">Strada Nuova</a> and the Rolli system of lodging state guests, now a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>, in its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baroque">Baroque</a> art, and in Genoese communities that shaped <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carloforte">Carloforte</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia.jpg" alt="War of Chioggia" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canal_Vena_in_Chioggia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Didier Descouens, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>’s decline was gradual. The loss of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-chioggia">War of Chioggia</a>, factional strife and foreign domination weakened it in the late fourteenth and fifteenth centuries, while the Ottoman advance stripped away its eastern empire: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galata">Pera</a> surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> in 1453, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> fell in 1475 and Chios in 1566. The Spanish alliance of 1528 gave the republic stability and great banking wealth, but it was a client of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, exposed to the repeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereign-default">bankruptcies</a> of the Spanish crown, the last of which in 1627 hit Genoese lenders hard. Plague in 1656–1657 killed a large part of the population. When Genoa built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galley">galleys</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> and resisted French pressure, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> had the city bombarded in 1684 and forced its doge to apologise in person at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> in 1685. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a> Austrian troops occupied Genoa in 1746 until a popular rising, traditionally begun by the boy known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balilla">Balilla</a>, drove them out. The long Corsican revolt from 1729, including the rule of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a> from 1755, led Genoa to cede the island to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> in 1768. In 1797 French pressure under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a> replaced the aristocratic constitution with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ligurian-republic">Ligurian Republic</a>; Genoa was annexed to France in 1805 and given to the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> in 1815.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> built a commercial empire from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> to the Atlantic, pioneered the contracts, insurance and public debt of European capitalism, and through its bankers financed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, making a small Ligurian city one of the makers of the early modern world economy.</em></p>

